Below is a minimal, self-contained example that shows how to “deny access” to any request that hits a web site or API.  
Pick the snippet that matches your stack and drop it in as-is; every caller will receive a generic **403 Forbidden** response (or 401/429 if you prefer).  
No dependencies beyond what the runtime already provides.

--------------------------------------------------
1. Apache (.htaccess) – blanket 403
--------------------------------------------------
# /var/www/html/.htaccess
# Deny every request that reaches this directory
Require all denied

--------------------------------------------------
2. Nginx – blanket 403
--------------------------------------------------
# /etc/nginx/conf.d/000-deny-all.conf
server {
    listen 80 default_server;
    listen [::]:80 default_server;
    location / {
        return 403;          # or 401 / 444 (drop) / 429
    }
}

--------------------------------------------------
3. Node.js (Express) – deny every route
--------------------------------------------------
// deny.js  –  node deny.js
const express = require('express');
const app = express();
const PORT = process.env.PORT || 3000;

app.use((req, res) => res.status(403).send('Forbidden'));

app.listen(PORT, () => console.log(`Denying every request on :${PORT}`));

--------------------------------------------------
4. Python (Flask) – deny every route
--------------------------------------------------
# deny.py
from flask import Flask, abort
app = Flask(__name__)

@app.route('/', defaults={'path': ''})
@app.route('/<path:path>')
def deny(_):
    abort(403)

if __name__ == '__main__':
    app.run(host='0.0.0.0', port=8000)

--------------------------------------------------
5. PHP – single-line drop-in
--------------------------------------------------
<?php
// index.php (or prepend to any existing file)
http_response_code(403);
die('Forbidden');

--------------------------------------------------
6. Go – tiny static denier
--------------------------------------------------
// deny.go  –  go run deny.go
package main

import "net/http"

func main() {
    http.HandleFunc("/", func(w http.ResponseWriter, r *http.Request) {
        http.Error(w, "Forbidden", http.StatusForbidden)
    })
    http.ListenAndServe(":8080", nil)
}

--------------------------------------------------
7. Cloudflare Worker (edge-level 403)
--------------------------------------------------
// Deploy at https://dash.cloudflare.com → Workers → Create Service
addEventListener('fetch', event => {
  event.respondWith(new Response('Forbidden', {status: 403}));
});

--------------------------------------------------
Usage notes / hardening
--------------------------------------------------
- Return 444 (Nginx) or simply drop the TCP connection if you want zero response bytes.  
- Combine with firewall rules (`iptables -A INPUT -p tcp --dport 80 -j DROP`) for lower-level blocking.  
- If you only want to block certain IPs, user-agents, or countries, swap the blanket deny for conditional logic.
